{
  "image_name": "67d800ffced7318ea23636d53899bf86.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares conventional alkaline water electrolysis with a modified scheme that temporarily stores the oxygen-forming reaction in a nickel hydroxide electrode. In the top panel, a standard two-electrode cell is shown with an anode, cathode, and membrane separator. Water is reduced at the cathode to produce hydrogen, while hydroxide ions move across the cell and are oxidized at the anode to release oxygen. Electrons flow through an external power supply between the two electrodes.\n\nIn the lower scheme, hydrogen is still produced at the cathode during electrolysis, but the usual oxygen evolution at the anode is replaced by a solid-state conversion of Ni(OH)2 to NiOOH. This means the cell can generate hydrogen without simultaneous oxygen bubbling at the anode. The diagram then shows a second step in which the charged NiOOH electrode is removed and treated with water and heat, reversing back to Ni(OH)2 while releasing oxygen separately. Overall, the figure illustrates a decoupled water-splitting process: hydrogen is generated electrochemically in one stage, and oxygen is produced later in a separate step, apparently eliminating the need for a membrane during hydrogen production.",
